1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,320
Today on the show, I sat down with SEC Commissioner Hester Peirce.

2
00:00:04,320 --> 00:00:07,640
Listeners of this show will know that we have long been big fans of Commissioner Peirce

3
00:00:07,640 --> 00:00:12,560
and her proactive work to promote pro-innovation policies in the blockchain sector.

4
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:17,080
In this episode, we discussed the past, the present, and the future of the SEC as it relates

5
00:00:17,080 --> 00:00:18,760
to blockchain technology.

6
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:23,000
We talked about the role of Congress and the path forward, and we talked about some tangible

7
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:26,040
advice for founders in the digital asset ecosystem.

8
00:00:26,040 --> 00:00:27,560
I think you'll enjoy this one.

9
00:00:27,560 --> 00:00:32,560
So without further ado, here's my conversation with SEC Commissioner Hester Peirce.

10
00:00:32,560 --> 00:00:35,560
Matt Walsh and Nick Carter are partners at Castle Island Ventures.

11
00:00:35,560 --> 00:00:38,160
All of these expressed by them or the guests on this podcast are solely their opinions

12
00:00:38,160 --> 00:00:40,720
and do not reflect the opinions of Castle Island Ventures.

13
00:00:40,720 --> 00:00:43,160
Guests and hosts may maintain positions in the assets discussed in this podcast.

14
00:00:43,160 --> 00:00:46,440
You should not treat any opinion expressed by anyone on this podcast as a specific inducement

15
00:00:46,440 --> 00:00:50,080
to make a particular investment or follow a particular strategy, but only as an expression

16
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:51,080
of their personal opinion.

17
00:00:51,080 --> 00:00:53,200
This podcast is for informational purposes only.

18
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:29,280
Commissioner Peirce, thank you so much for coming on the podcast.

19
00:01:29,280 --> 00:01:31,040
I feel like this has been a long time coming.

20
00:01:31,040 --> 00:01:32,840
We've wanted to have you on for quite a while.

21
00:01:32,840 --> 00:01:34,880
Well, Matt, it's a pleasure to be here.

22
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:36,880
I am a big follower of your show.

23
00:01:36,880 --> 00:01:38,400
I love to listen to it.

24
00:01:38,400 --> 00:01:41,680
I of course have to give you my disclaimer, which is that my views are my own views of

25
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:45,360
this commissioner, not necessarily those of the SEC or my fellow commissioners.

26
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:46,360
Awesome.

27
00:01:46,360 --> 00:01:48,960
I'm used to hearing that disclaimer in no surprise.

28
00:01:48,960 --> 00:01:53,120
I'd love to just start this maybe with a personal question of, what has it been like

29
00:01:53,120 --> 00:01:56,040
for you to work at the SEC for the past few years?

30
00:01:56,040 --> 00:01:59,960
Obviously, there are a lot of great people there and I enjoy working there, but from

31
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:04,880
the perspective of crypto policy, it's been extremely frustrating because I feel like

32
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:10,920
it's been one bad decision after another, so yeah, I'm happy that we're in a new day.

33
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:12,120
You said you listened to the podcast.

34
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:15,760
You might have heard us say this before, but I'm kind of surprised that you're still there

35
00:02:15,760 --> 00:02:18,840
given everything that you've had to push through at the SEC.

36
00:02:18,840 --> 00:02:21,080
Do you ever consider leaving the agency?

37
00:02:21,080 --> 00:02:22,080
Yeah.

38
00:02:22,080 --> 00:02:25,600
I mean, I've been there for a long time and I thought that it would be good to have a

39
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:29,440
new voice come in there, so that certainly has crossed my mind.

40
00:02:29,440 --> 00:02:35,000
The reason that I'm staying on for a little bit now is I really am looking forward to

41
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:36,440
Paul Atkins arriving.

42
00:02:36,440 --> 00:02:37,840
I worked for him.

43
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:42,680
I know how he thinks and I think it will be good for the agency and good for capital

44
00:02:42,680 --> 00:02:44,080
markets.

45
00:02:44,080 --> 00:02:48,880
In an ideal state, I'd be curious how the agency is really meant to function with regards

46
00:02:48,880 --> 00:02:52,240
to dissents around different commissioners having different points of view.

47
00:02:52,240 --> 00:02:53,800
Is that a collaborative process?

48
00:02:53,800 --> 00:02:55,560
Does that happen in a vacuum?

49
00:02:55,560 --> 00:02:57,840
How should that work in principle?

50
00:02:57,840 --> 00:03:01,280
I think that it's a five-member commission for a reason.

51
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:06,320
You want to have different people with different perspectives on things, and so it's natural

52
00:03:06,320 --> 00:03:09,760
that you're going to get some dissents, concurrences, and things like that.

53
00:03:09,760 --> 00:03:16,040
When we do get along as people and as colleagues, we get along with each other so we can share

54
00:03:16,040 --> 00:03:21,400
our thoughts about things, and I'm a little predictable on some of these things, so it's

55
00:03:21,400 --> 00:03:25,960
no surprise that I'm going to be dissenting on some of these things.

56
00:03:25,960 --> 00:03:32,200
I think that we could do a better job maybe learning from each other sometimes.

57
00:03:32,200 --> 00:03:36,960
We all have something to learn from one another on these issues, but I really felt, and I

58
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:39,920
think you captured that when you said, what's it like to be there?

59
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:44,680
It really did sometimes feel like it was hard to get people to pay attention, and that's

60
00:03:44,680 --> 00:03:45,680
probably on me.

61
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:50,720
If I had done a better job making my points, maybe we would be in the position four years

62
00:03:50,720 --> 00:03:56,080
ago of doing something proactive instead of just continuing year after year not to do

63
00:03:56,080 --> 00:03:57,720
anything proactive.

64
00:03:57,720 --> 00:04:01,440
When I had gotten started in this industry initially when I was working at Fidelity back

65
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:06,880
in 2014, it always struck me that there would be a fair amount of education necessary to

66
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:11,160
really talk to the regulators, explain how the technology works, explain some of the

67
00:04:11,160 --> 00:04:17,040
product ideas, and as that went forward, I started to notice that some of these regulators

68
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:22,200
really start to get it, you being the leading one really with the initial Safe Harbor proposal.

69
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:26,800
I'm sort of at a loss as to why we ended up in this situation in some respects with

70
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:29,480
the SEC, and I get that question a lot.

71
00:04:29,480 --> 00:04:35,160
Why did the agency try to prevent some of these companies from getting off the ground

72
00:04:35,320 --> 00:04:38,400
and some of the intermediaries from actually accessing this market?

73
00:04:38,400 --> 00:04:39,960
I'm wondering if you can speak to that.

74
00:04:39,960 --> 00:04:42,680
I mean, I think it's kind of a multi-stranded thing.

75
00:04:42,680 --> 00:04:48,360
One is that there was and is a lot of bad activity that is being perpetrated either

76
00:04:48,360 --> 00:04:54,000
in crypto, in the name of crypto, so that scares people, and understandably so.

77
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,480
A regulator is going to look at that and say, why do I want that in my jurisdiction at

78
00:04:57,480 --> 00:04:58,480
all?

79
00:04:58,480 --> 00:05:00,320
I'd rather just shut that down.

80
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:04,880
I think it is hard to see through that bad activity, and then there have been a lot of

81
00:05:04,920 --> 00:05:08,440
promises about all the great things that crypto was going to do.

82
00:05:08,440 --> 00:05:14,600
And many of those things, as with any new technology that's being tried and applied,

83
00:05:14,600 --> 00:05:15,760
it doesn't all work.

84
00:05:15,760 --> 00:05:17,600
So of course, some things aren't going to work.

85
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:22,280
So if you see the bad conduct and you're not seeing these massive changes, I think that

86
00:05:22,280 --> 00:05:26,560
leads to saying, well, whatever, we're just going to use the regulatory process to kind

87
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:27,960
of shut this down.

88
00:05:27,960 --> 00:05:34,480
I was enthusiastic that Chair Gensler would come in, and he had taught about this at MIT.

89
00:05:34,520 --> 00:05:39,880
I was thinking, we're going to have someone who really does understand this and really

90
00:05:39,880 --> 00:05:42,560
can figure out a good regulatory regime.

91
00:05:42,560 --> 00:05:46,800
He had built a regulatory regime for swaps when he was at the CFTC.

92
00:05:46,800 --> 00:05:50,800
I didn't love everything about that regime, but he had stood up a regime.

93
00:05:51,400 --> 00:05:55,200
In my experience, it didn't seem he found this technology so interesting.

94
00:05:55,520 --> 00:06:00,360
Frankly, you guys talk a lot about Operation Choke Point, and I think there are different

95
00:06:00,360 --> 00:06:02,920
ways for Operation Choke Point to manifest itself.

96
00:06:02,920 --> 00:06:09,360
And one way is, you can just come in with regulations and you can make it really unattractive

97
00:06:09,360 --> 00:06:12,080
for people to participate in the industry.

98
00:06:12,320 --> 00:06:17,160
You're always talking about Sab-121, or you were, which was helpful for me.

99
00:06:17,320 --> 00:06:21,760
I thought it was a problem, but you and others really underscored for me that it was a problem.

100
00:06:21,760 --> 00:06:24,240
And that's a way of keeping legitimate actors out.

101
00:06:24,240 --> 00:06:29,240
So I feel like it's sort of a confluence of events that just led to this really negative spiral.

102
00:06:29,680 --> 00:06:34,320
You hit on it and wouldn't be a Castle Island podcast without talking about Sab-121.

103
00:06:34,320 --> 00:06:38,040
But when I really start back and reflect on the past few years, I totally agree with you

104
00:06:38,040 --> 00:06:39,040
on the bad behavior.

105
00:06:39,040 --> 00:06:43,200
In a lot of ways, I was hoping that the SEC would be a little bit more active, or maybe

106
00:06:43,200 --> 00:06:47,160
it's the DOJ, and cutting down on some of these unregulated offshore venues.

107
00:06:47,160 --> 00:06:49,160
I think that would have been healthier for the industry.

108
00:06:49,480 --> 00:06:51,880
Same thing for ICOs, actually, in the earlier days.

109
00:06:52,280 --> 00:06:55,840
Some of the clearly unregulated securities offerings that were happening.

110
00:06:56,440 --> 00:07:02,600
But when I look at the past two or three years, Sab-121 inclusive of this, it does feel like

111
00:07:02,600 --> 00:07:05,920
there is a bigger force at play to just choke off the industry.

112
00:07:06,040 --> 00:07:08,400
You have the debanking on the banking regulator side.

113
00:07:08,400 --> 00:07:09,920
We were seeing that left and right.

114
00:07:10,240 --> 00:07:14,200
And then with respect to the SEC, if you can prevent the banks from coming in and

115
00:07:14,280 --> 00:07:18,800
custodying these assets and being active in stablecoins, that is just a great way to

116
00:07:18,800 --> 00:07:22,840
stymie innovation and prevent startups who want to sell into those institutions from

117
00:07:22,840 --> 00:07:23,720
getting off the ground.

118
00:07:24,200 --> 00:07:28,480
Is it as easy as just the leadership at the SEC not wanting to do anything with the

119
00:07:28,480 --> 00:07:33,840
technology, or is there a bigger political force here that was trying to shut this down?

120
00:07:34,320 --> 00:07:34,960
I don't know.

121
00:07:35,000 --> 00:07:36,520
We can speculate about that.

122
00:07:36,520 --> 00:07:41,640
I mean, I think there's a lot of pressure to have a financial system that's very

123
00:07:41,640 --> 00:07:47,280
intermediated, because it's very easy to, easy is not the right word, but it's easier to

124
00:07:47,280 --> 00:07:52,360
regulate a financial system that has a lot of very large intermediaries, because you

125
00:07:52,360 --> 00:07:53,920
know who to go to when there's a problem.

126
00:07:54,320 --> 00:07:55,600
So maybe there's some of that.

127
00:07:55,800 --> 00:08:00,680
I think that a lot of people's motives, even people who are not favorable toward

128
00:08:00,680 --> 00:08:02,320
crypto are good motives.

129
00:08:02,600 --> 00:08:04,200
There may be bad motives too.

130
00:08:04,200 --> 00:08:04,800
I don't know.

131
00:08:05,200 --> 00:08:08,080
I think the better thing to focus on is how can we change that?

132
00:08:08,400 --> 00:08:12,920
But it's not only something like SAB 121, but it's also how do we interact with

133
00:08:12,920 --> 00:08:17,000
investment advisors who have some crypto in their portfolio?

134
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:21,680
How do we interact with auditors that are trying to figure out how to do proof of reserves?

135
00:08:21,920 --> 00:08:24,560
There are lots of different ways that we interact with this.

136
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:27,120
And so I think a lot of it was negative.

137
00:08:27,120 --> 00:08:28,600
I hope we can turn it positive.

138
00:08:29,080 --> 00:08:34,360
I will just say in response to your wish that the SEC had been more active in some ways.

139
00:08:34,760 --> 00:08:37,400
One is, and I'm always harping about this.

140
00:08:37,680 --> 00:08:39,200
I think it's really important I say this.

141
00:08:39,440 --> 00:08:41,360
We do have jurisdictional boundaries.

142
00:08:41,560 --> 00:08:43,720
We were supposed to focus on activity in the US.

143
00:08:44,200 --> 00:08:48,360
And there are some instances when we're looking at stuff outside that has an

144
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:53,680
effect in the US, but really the goal is for us to regulate here.

145
00:08:53,680 --> 00:08:59,320
And so I think what I find so sad is that if we had set up a good regulatory regime

146
00:08:59,320 --> 00:09:02,960
in the US, people probably wouldn't have done stuff outside the US.

147
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:06,640
And if they had, people might have said, well, why aren't you in the US where there's

148
00:09:06,640 --> 00:09:11,880
a good regulatory regime and we can rely on the fact that there's some regulation there?

149
00:09:12,200 --> 00:09:17,080
And so I think we pushed stuff out of the US that didn't end up helping American people.

150
00:09:17,280 --> 00:09:18,960
And we wasted a lot of time.

151
00:09:19,280 --> 00:09:24,240
And then with respect to ICOs, yes, I mean, some activity is securities activity.

152
00:09:24,240 --> 00:09:29,280
But again, you've got to build some kind of a framework that works for people to do

153
00:09:29,280 --> 00:09:30,440
those kinds of things.

154
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:34,200
And you might be able to do the initial offering through one of the existing

155
00:09:34,200 --> 00:09:38,640
exemptions, but because there was no long-term way for getting those tokens out

156
00:09:38,640 --> 00:09:42,800
into the broader world, it just, again, cultivates bad activity.

157
00:09:43,280 --> 00:09:44,920
Yeah, I think that's spot on.

158
00:09:44,920 --> 00:09:49,200
On your first point on the unregulated offshore venues, there was a time, it was a

159
00:09:49,200 --> 00:09:52,960
few years ago now before they collapsed, but there was a time when I actually would

160
00:09:52,960 --> 00:09:56,400
say that FTX was maybe our biggest mess because when we had looked at some of the

161
00:09:56,400 --> 00:10:01,440
early rounds, we said, well, this is an unregulated offshore venue, clearly serving

162
00:10:01,480 --> 00:10:04,760
US citizens with securities on the platform.

163
00:10:05,040 --> 00:10:06,880
And they also run this market-making firm.

164
00:10:06,960 --> 00:10:08,600
This is clearly a conflict of interest.

165
00:10:08,880 --> 00:10:12,600
There's going to be US alternatives here that pop up, but there didn't end up

166
00:10:12,600 --> 00:10:13,160
being that.

167
00:10:13,160 --> 00:10:15,160
And so it ended up for a short period of time.

168
00:10:15,160 --> 00:10:17,080
We're lucky we didn't invest in it, I suppose.

169
00:10:17,080 --> 00:10:20,720
But it's interesting to think through just the deployment of capital and how

170
00:10:20,720 --> 00:10:24,200
that's been altered by the ambiguity in the regulatory climate.

171
00:10:24,200 --> 00:10:25,680
Yeah, absolutely.

172
00:10:25,800 --> 00:10:31,120
That's really scary for a regulator to see that, to see if you do your job badly,

173
00:10:31,120 --> 00:10:34,200
you can contribute to some really bad things happening.

174
00:10:34,560 --> 00:10:38,320
And that's been a little bit the lesson of my career because I was around

175
00:10:38,320 --> 00:10:42,320
during, obviously, the financial crisis of 2008.

176
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:45,000
And I think some of that stem too from bad regulation.

177
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:48,440
So it is definitely daunting when you think about that.

178
00:10:49,040 --> 00:10:52,840
One of the things I'd love to get your take on is just the role of Congress in

179
00:10:52,840 --> 00:10:56,600
defining what a market structure should look like here versus the role of the

180
00:10:56,600 --> 00:11:00,240
SEC and maybe a collaborative relationship with the CFTC.

181
00:11:00,720 --> 00:11:04,920
How much can you do at the SEC here and where does Congress need to step in?

182
00:11:05,360 --> 00:11:09,200
I mean, I think we can do a lot initially just saying, here's what's within

183
00:11:09,200 --> 00:11:10,920
our jurisdiction, here's what's outside.

184
00:11:10,920 --> 00:11:14,360
And then if it's within, we can write some rules for that.

185
00:11:14,640 --> 00:11:20,080
I do think at the end of the day, Congress will want, I mean, it does want to

186
00:11:20,080 --> 00:11:24,360
come in and allocate jurisdiction for the stuff that I don't think is really

187
00:11:24,360 --> 00:11:25,880
covered by anyone now.

188
00:11:26,320 --> 00:11:30,600
So if you have platforms that are trading things that are not securities

189
00:11:31,160 --> 00:11:33,960
and they're not futures, where do they fit?

190
00:11:34,400 --> 00:11:38,760
That's where Congress is looking to try to figure out who that should go to.

191
00:11:38,760 --> 00:11:39,600
Is it the CFTC?

192
00:11:39,600 --> 00:11:40,200
Is it us?

193
00:11:40,480 --> 00:11:45,000
In the interim, the CFTC and we can work together to help do some of the

194
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:50,600
groundwork, what's help them understand what the landscape is and where regulatory

195
00:11:50,600 --> 00:11:52,960
authority might make the most sense.

196
00:11:52,960 --> 00:11:55,280
But ultimately, obviously, that's going to be their call.

197
00:11:55,720 --> 00:11:57,320
So I think we can work in parallel.

198
00:11:57,760 --> 00:12:02,200
Now that we are sort of taking a more proactive look, I think our technical

199
00:12:02,200 --> 00:12:04,760
assistance to Congress can be more valuable as well.

200
00:12:05,280 --> 00:12:09,120
Once we understand what Congress wants to do, we can tell them whether this

201
00:12:09,120 --> 00:12:13,200
will fit with how we look at traditional securities markets.

202
00:12:13,200 --> 00:12:16,200
And I think that can be helpful as I try to frame this out.

203
00:12:16,200 --> 00:12:18,280
I mean, it's not an easy task for them.

204
00:12:18,720 --> 00:12:21,760
Definitely not an easy task, but it does seem like there's just an order

205
00:12:21,760 --> 00:12:25,680
of magnitude, more engagement and knowledge in this current Congress than

206
00:12:25,680 --> 00:12:28,560
there was in the past one, particularly in the financial services committees.

207
00:12:29,040 --> 00:12:30,680
Yeah, I mean, there's a lot of knowledge.

208
00:12:30,680 --> 00:12:33,720
I'm impressed that people who have so many other things to do have time

209
00:12:33,720 --> 00:12:34,880
to figure this stuff out.

210
00:12:35,280 --> 00:12:38,520
The other thing we have to be thinking about is we're looking at the industry

211
00:12:38,560 --> 00:12:42,200
as it is now, but we also want to make sure that whatever we build is going

212
00:12:42,200 --> 00:12:45,720
to work in the long term, because it's really hard to change legislation,

213
00:12:45,840 --> 00:12:48,560
hard to change statutes, hard to change regulations.

214
00:12:49,200 --> 00:12:52,920
When I talk to institutional allocators that are deploying to funds,

215
00:12:53,160 --> 00:12:55,200
they always ask around the use cases.

216
00:12:55,200 --> 00:12:59,280
So within crypto, you clearly have a store of value use case, Bitcoin

217
00:12:59,280 --> 00:13:00,240
being the leader there.

218
00:13:00,440 --> 00:13:04,360
You clearly have this open source operating system use case, the smart

219
00:13:04,360 --> 00:13:07,440
contract platforms, Ethereum, Solana, Monad, whatnot.

220
00:13:07,640 --> 00:13:11,200
And then stablecoins is just clearly a breakout killer app where I think

221
00:13:11,200 --> 00:13:15,360
it's 70% of every transaction on a blockchain is a US dollar moving.

222
00:13:15,360 --> 00:13:18,800
You see international remittances is popping up as a great use case.

223
00:13:19,240 --> 00:13:23,120
But there are so many more use cases that could be there if there was

224
00:13:23,120 --> 00:13:26,280
regulatory clarity on how to bring one of these things to market.

225
00:13:26,760 --> 00:13:30,920
And so many companies have not gotten started over the past four years

226
00:13:31,040 --> 00:13:34,600
because of that, because of just the personal risk that the entrepreneur

227
00:13:34,600 --> 00:13:36,960
would need to take and all of the brain damage.

228
00:13:37,360 --> 00:13:40,480
And I think in a lot of ways, these things would have to be some sort

229
00:13:40,480 --> 00:13:43,080
of a security out of the gate to raise capital.

230
00:13:43,160 --> 00:13:46,560
And then the token itself, obviously, hopefully would not be at some point.

231
00:13:46,560 --> 00:13:50,040
But you were onto this really early with the safe harbor proposal.

232
00:13:50,080 --> 00:13:53,760
Curious how you're thinking has maybe changed over time or do you still

233
00:13:53,760 --> 00:13:56,080
think that could be something that we put into place?

234
00:13:56,560 --> 00:14:00,080
Yeah, I mean, I think something like that could work just to make sure

235
00:14:00,080 --> 00:14:05,440
that people have a way to get the ecosystem working, get people using it

236
00:14:05,640 --> 00:14:09,320
without having the fear of the securities law overhang, but also

237
00:14:09,320 --> 00:14:12,840
keeping a little pressure on people to actually keep doing something,

238
00:14:13,040 --> 00:14:15,600
not just launch the tokens and then run away.

239
00:14:16,040 --> 00:14:20,560
I'm looking forward to what people have to say about an approach like that.

240
00:14:20,880 --> 00:14:24,440
I think we can design something like that, certainly for the short term,

241
00:14:24,440 --> 00:14:27,800
and then trying to figure out if that's really the right long term solution

242
00:14:27,800 --> 00:14:29,280
or if there's some better solution.

243
00:14:29,480 --> 00:14:34,000
I think we do have to think about the complexity of if you have something

244
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:39,000
where something is going to morph from a security to a non-security,

245
00:14:39,320 --> 00:14:42,680
that is going to raise a lot of questions around where is the line?

246
00:14:42,800 --> 00:14:44,280
When does that change happen?

247
00:14:44,680 --> 00:14:47,960
You don't want to have to switch trading platforms because

248
00:14:47,960 --> 00:14:50,800
something has switched from a security to a non-security.

249
00:14:51,080 --> 00:14:55,120
So I think if we focus on the fact that the token itself is probably

250
00:14:55,120 --> 00:14:59,920
often not the security, I mean, it may be sold as part of an investment contract

251
00:14:59,920 --> 00:15:05,320
and that's fine, but the token itself, unless it has security-like characteristics,

252
00:15:05,840 --> 00:15:06,880
probably is not.

253
00:15:06,880 --> 00:15:10,360
I think that might help us not have to draw those lines.

254
00:15:10,840 --> 00:15:11,400
I don't know.

255
00:15:11,400 --> 00:15:12,040
We'll see.

256
00:15:12,040 --> 00:15:15,360
I'm looking forward to just getting a lot of input on that point.

257
00:15:15,720 --> 00:15:21,480
Unfortunately, we've really muddled the discussion by not being very legally

258
00:15:21,480 --> 00:15:25,160
precise by going out there and saying, well, we think all of these tokens

259
00:15:25,160 --> 00:15:30,360
are securities when I don't know that, I mean, I certainly don't look at it that way.

260
00:15:30,600 --> 00:15:35,400
It's unusual to have investment contracts that trade on secondary markets.

261
00:15:35,400 --> 00:15:37,880
So this is all a little bit of a new territory for us.

262
00:15:38,520 --> 00:15:42,080
Our interpretation of the Howie test has been very broad in my view.

263
00:15:42,680 --> 00:15:46,360
So we've got to figure it out, but I think the safe harbor is a place

264
00:15:46,360 --> 00:15:48,280
that the discussion can at least start.

265
00:15:48,800 --> 00:15:50,360
Yeah, I think it was a great starting point.

266
00:15:50,560 --> 00:15:53,640
You brought up disclosures in your answer there and that's something

267
00:15:53,640 --> 00:15:58,000
that was just totally eye-opening to me during the ICO phenomenon that you would

268
00:15:58,000 --> 00:16:02,000
dive into the actual network data and you'd look at the addresses of the core

269
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:06,680
team or maybe some of the early investors and you would see really bad behavior or

270
00:16:06,680 --> 00:16:10,600
you would see something in a white paper that was just not represented on chain.

271
00:16:11,040 --> 00:16:14,600
So I've always thought that it would be a great opportunity to build a disclosure

272
00:16:14,600 --> 00:16:17,400
framework, first of all, for the government maybe to have one.

273
00:16:17,720 --> 00:16:21,480
But I'm also curious if there's a role for private industry in pushing

274
00:16:21,480 --> 00:16:23,200
something like that as a standard.

275
00:16:23,520 --> 00:16:27,200
How do you think that could evolve in terms of just disclosing ownership,

276
00:16:27,200 --> 00:16:29,040
investing schedules and things like that?

277
00:16:29,640 --> 00:16:30,840
I think that would be great.

278
00:16:30,960 --> 00:16:33,360
I think it would benefit everyone to have that out there.

279
00:16:33,840 --> 00:16:38,400
And this is another example of where regulation has actually discouraged disclosure.

280
00:16:38,880 --> 00:16:43,240
So now I think the industry may be more empowered to come up with some kind of

281
00:16:43,240 --> 00:16:47,840
standard and we can bake that into regulation to the extent we think that

282
00:16:47,840 --> 00:16:50,160
it's evergreen, that it's not going to change a lot.

283
00:16:50,160 --> 00:16:53,200
And I think some of the things that you raised are exactly the kind of thing I

284
00:16:53,200 --> 00:16:53,960
want to get to.

285
00:16:53,960 --> 00:16:58,720
I mean, people need to understand that the white paper actually is consistent with

286
00:16:58,720 --> 00:16:59,240
the code.

287
00:16:59,600 --> 00:17:03,720
They need to understand what the development team is thinking about.

288
00:17:04,080 --> 00:17:09,520
Is this really just an opportunity for them to very opportunistically dump a lot

289
00:17:09,520 --> 00:17:12,960
of tokens on people and then just run and abandon things?

290
00:17:13,440 --> 00:17:19,480
I'm really excited to see now that we have a more positive regulatory approach to

291
00:17:19,480 --> 00:17:23,400
see good behavior being rewarded and bad behavior being punished.

292
00:17:23,800 --> 00:17:24,640
Yeah, me too.

293
00:17:24,720 --> 00:17:28,800
I don't know if does that end up looking like an Edgar system for crypto assets

294
00:17:28,800 --> 00:17:30,280
or how do you think this could evolve?

295
00:17:30,640 --> 00:17:34,440
Maybe it's just telling people you've got to make these disclosures and they have

296
00:17:34,440 --> 00:17:36,000
to be available to the public.

297
00:17:36,160 --> 00:17:40,040
I don't know that we need to set up a formal Edgar system because again,

298
00:17:40,040 --> 00:17:44,400
whatever we build, it needs to recognize that a lot of these teams are very small

299
00:17:44,960 --> 00:17:47,040
and they're not going to have lots of lawyers.

300
00:17:47,040 --> 00:17:49,600
They may not have any in-house lawyers.

301
00:17:49,600 --> 00:17:53,520
They may be relying on outside counsel and they can't pay a lot for that.

302
00:17:53,520 --> 00:17:56,360
So I want to make sure whatever it is, it's something that's doable.

303
00:17:56,760 --> 00:18:00,680
And I think we can probably look at how things are going in Europe to see how

304
00:18:00,680 --> 00:18:05,160
their disclosure system is working and whether that's helpful to us.

305
00:18:05,560 --> 00:18:06,520
Yeah, that's interesting.

306
00:18:06,520 --> 00:18:09,800
I think Mika certainly could be a model that could be explored.

307
00:18:09,960 --> 00:18:14,160
Although I was looking at one of those disclosures the other day and they had a

308
00:18:14,200 --> 00:18:18,520
lot about scope three, greenhouse gas emissions and things like that.

309
00:18:18,920 --> 00:18:22,240
That's not really where I want to spend our disclosure efforts.

310
00:18:22,480 --> 00:18:25,800
Do you think the SEC will actually just focus on capital markets and

311
00:18:26,320 --> 00:18:28,600
maybe that, yeah, that'd be nice.

312
00:18:28,880 --> 00:18:32,560
You brought up the role of lawyers and it strikes me that the lawyers have been

313
00:18:32,560 --> 00:18:33,800
doing great in this market.

314
00:18:33,960 --> 00:18:37,400
I mean, there's just a lot of defense attorneys and probably the SEC has

315
00:18:37,400 --> 00:18:40,280
spent a fair bit of money on some of these cases.

316
00:18:40,280 --> 00:18:44,680
So I guess just a general question around how should the agency really function

317
00:18:44,760 --> 00:18:48,640
when it comes to spending taxpayer dollars prosecuting some of these things

318
00:18:48,640 --> 00:18:50,600
that are to say the least, like not very clear.

319
00:18:51,000 --> 00:18:54,880
Yeah, I mean, I think that's the big question for us is how do we spend our

320
00:18:54,880 --> 00:18:59,320
regulatory resources and it does matter which tool you use for what?

321
00:18:59,320 --> 00:19:02,520
Because litigation is very expensive for us.

322
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:06,200
It is certainly expensive for people that we're litigating against.

323
00:19:06,600 --> 00:19:11,280
And so it's a tool we use, but we should use it judiciously and we shouldn't be

324
00:19:11,280 --> 00:19:16,520
using enforcement actions to try to write rules, which we were absolutely trying to do.

325
00:19:16,960 --> 00:19:18,280
That's not good for anyone.

326
00:19:18,360 --> 00:19:19,400
It's expensive.

327
00:19:19,440 --> 00:19:23,760
You've got people at the SEC who are experts in policy and they're not at the

328
00:19:23,760 --> 00:19:27,120
table when you're having an enforcement settlement negotiation with someone.

329
00:19:27,440 --> 00:19:30,920
They may be consulted, but they're not driving that negotiation.

330
00:19:31,240 --> 00:19:32,760
And same on the outside.

331
00:19:32,760 --> 00:19:35,480
I mean, you hire litigators to litigate these cases.

332
00:19:35,480 --> 00:19:38,880
You're not hiring a regulatory lawyer to do this.

333
00:19:39,200 --> 00:19:43,000
So it's just been a really bad use of resources.

334
00:19:43,320 --> 00:19:48,240
And as a result, we haven't had as many resources to go after the truly bad actors.

335
00:19:48,240 --> 00:19:52,840
Now, some of that stuff, again, may not be within our jurisdiction, but we can be

336
00:19:52,840 --> 00:19:53,920
on the lookout for it.

337
00:19:53,920 --> 00:19:56,240
We can be referring it to other regulators.

338
00:19:56,760 --> 00:20:01,640
I think if we get to a better place, regulatory will have more enforcement

339
00:20:01,640 --> 00:20:04,000
resources to use in better ways.

340
00:20:04,440 --> 00:20:05,520
That makes total sense.

341
00:20:05,720 --> 00:20:09,120
One of the areas that I've been interested in for a long time, but we haven't

342
00:20:09,120 --> 00:20:13,040
deployed a lot of capital against is this idea of tokenized securities.

343
00:20:13,120 --> 00:20:17,840
So things that maybe exist in the real world or net new instruments that are

344
00:20:17,840 --> 00:20:19,920
online, but they're actually securities.

345
00:20:19,920 --> 00:20:24,000
They want to be securities and been hard for us from an investment perspective

346
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:25,960
because we see a lot of really good ideas.

347
00:20:26,280 --> 00:20:30,000
But there are clear regulatory impediments to some of these things working in terms

348
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:33,040
of secondary markets and the definition of custody.

349
00:20:33,400 --> 00:20:34,640
How are you thinking about that?

350
00:20:34,640 --> 00:20:38,640
And I know that you've put out a really great piece around the journey begins,

351
00:20:38,640 --> 00:20:40,000
which is February 4th.

352
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,440
You put up this statement and it does touch on this.

353
00:20:42,440 --> 00:20:44,720
So I'm curious how you're thinking about that category.

354
00:20:45,360 --> 00:20:45,600
Yeah.

355
00:20:45,600 --> 00:20:49,440
I mean, I think it will be an area that we see a lot of people trying to

356
00:20:49,440 --> 00:20:50,800
innovate and experiment.

357
00:20:51,200 --> 00:20:55,880
I think it hasn't really happened very much because anything that had any kind

358
00:20:55,880 --> 00:21:00,920
of mention of tokenization or any kind of mention of blockchain, I think people

359
00:21:00,920 --> 00:21:03,800
were scared to engage with us about that.

360
00:21:04,080 --> 00:21:07,080
There's been some experimentation and I think that's great.

361
00:21:07,080 --> 00:21:09,120
We've seen money market funds tokenized.

362
00:21:09,400 --> 00:21:13,520
I think people have started to step into it, but I expect we'll see a lot more of that.

363
00:21:13,800 --> 00:21:14,840
I don't know where it will go.

364
00:21:14,840 --> 00:21:17,440
I think it's exciting to think about the possibilities.

365
00:21:17,520 --> 00:21:22,280
You can take a bond and if you can tokenize it, you can make sure that all the data

366
00:21:22,320 --> 00:21:26,680
travels with that bond instead of having a lot of documents on the side.

367
00:21:26,920 --> 00:21:30,640
I think that it could be more efficient in a lot of areas, but I'm not the

368
00:21:30,720 --> 00:21:31,640
technologist.

369
00:21:31,680 --> 00:21:35,240
I'll leave that to you guys to figure out what's going to work and what's not.

370
00:21:35,240 --> 00:21:39,640
But I think it certainly will see some more movement in the US on that.

371
00:21:40,160 --> 00:21:43,640
One of the things I wrestle with as a venture investor with this category is let's

372
00:21:43,640 --> 00:21:47,120
say that there was a framework in place and hopefully there is soon.

373
00:21:47,400 --> 00:21:50,880
And so all of the centralized institutions can do this type of activity,

374
00:21:50,880 --> 00:21:52,840
tokenized securities, moving it around.

375
00:21:53,280 --> 00:21:57,560
I completely understand how that could reduce cost at some of these institutions.

376
00:21:57,560 --> 00:22:00,240
There'd be potentially net new financial instruments.

377
00:22:00,760 --> 00:22:05,880
It's not obvious to me what the venture scale startup opportunities are necessarily

378
00:22:05,880 --> 00:22:07,200
in some of those categories.

379
00:22:07,440 --> 00:22:12,120
But if you could actually take some of these securities and interface with public

380
00:22:12,120 --> 00:22:16,160
blockchains in a DeFi context, I think that could get really interesting.

381
00:22:16,200 --> 00:22:20,120
Do you think that's a bridge too far to figure that part out right away or could

382
00:22:20,120 --> 00:22:21,040
that be part of this?

383
00:22:21,240 --> 00:22:22,920
I hope people will be thinking about that.

384
00:22:23,120 --> 00:22:27,440
You guys talk all the time about public versus permissioned blockchains.

385
00:22:27,760 --> 00:22:31,360
The benefit comes from having a public blockchain and figuring out what you can

386
00:22:31,360 --> 00:22:32,320
build on top of it.

387
00:22:32,320 --> 00:22:37,280
So I think that's what I'm excited to see people experimenting with.

388
00:22:37,440 --> 00:22:42,160
And there'll be a lot of need to educate us regulators on a lot of things.

389
00:22:42,160 --> 00:22:46,080
I think this is going to be an area where regulators will have to be able to get

390
00:22:46,080 --> 00:22:47,320
comfortable with this stuff.

391
00:22:47,360 --> 00:22:51,960
But I think that for me is where I think a lot of the really interesting

392
00:22:51,960 --> 00:22:53,320
innovation will happen.

393
00:22:53,320 --> 00:22:57,520
Again, I look more to people who actually are thinking about this all the time

394
00:22:57,520 --> 00:23:01,680
and investing, putting their money where their mouth is to see what really is

395
00:23:01,680 --> 00:23:03,040
going to take off and what won't.

396
00:23:03,440 --> 00:23:06,880
Yeah, it's instructive that at least stable coins work in this context.

397
00:23:06,880 --> 00:23:09,440
And I would argue a lot of those are not securities.

398
00:23:09,440 --> 00:23:11,240
Maybe some of them end up being who knows.

399
00:23:11,240 --> 00:23:15,560
But just to show what you can do with a US dollar and to extend that idea to

400
00:23:15,560 --> 00:23:16,600
other types of assets.

401
00:23:16,600 --> 00:23:19,480
I think that's the design space that a lot of entrepreneurs would like to

402
00:23:19,480 --> 00:23:20,000
explore.

403
00:23:20,480 --> 00:23:24,520
And the first step would be getting the centralized secondary trading venues up

404
00:23:24,520 --> 00:23:24,960
and running.

405
00:23:24,960 --> 00:23:28,160
But I think the next question for a lot of people is, well, what else can I do

406
00:23:28,160 --> 00:23:28,600
with this?

407
00:23:28,800 --> 00:23:31,280
Can I have this as a collateral asset in a DeFi pool?

408
00:23:31,720 --> 00:23:33,800
It'll be fun to watch what happens.

409
00:23:34,240 --> 00:23:39,360
Just flipping this back towards my side, what should we be thinking about as

410
00:23:39,360 --> 00:23:42,680
investors in this space and what should we be advising entrepreneurs?

411
00:23:42,720 --> 00:23:46,400
There was a time when coming in register was was offered to us.

412
00:23:46,400 --> 00:23:48,840
And it turns out that was a really, really bad idea.

413
00:23:48,880 --> 00:23:50,040
Yeah, I'm sorry.

414
00:23:50,240 --> 00:23:52,480
Yeah, what should we be telling our entrepreneurs?

415
00:23:52,840 --> 00:23:57,040
We really do need people to come in and talk to us and try to figure out what

416
00:23:57,200 --> 00:24:00,520
registration would look like if that's the right thing or what can we carve

417
00:24:00,520 --> 00:24:04,760
out that clearly isn't within our space and how can we provide clarity to

418
00:24:04,760 --> 00:24:07,400
people so that they can be comfortable?

419
00:24:07,640 --> 00:24:11,560
Because one of the things I've seen over the past several years is that people

420
00:24:11,560 --> 00:24:14,880
have said to me, Hey, Hester, there's something that I would like to do, but

421
00:24:14,880 --> 00:24:18,400
I'm concerned that you might come back five years later and say there's a

422
00:24:18,400 --> 00:24:19,280
securities angle.

423
00:24:19,280 --> 00:24:21,640
I actually don't think there is, but you might tell me there is.

424
00:24:21,640 --> 00:24:23,520
And so people have held off.

425
00:24:23,840 --> 00:24:28,240
I guess it would be helpful for me for people to come in and say, here are

426
00:24:28,240 --> 00:24:32,520
areas that would be helpful for you to make clear or not within your jurisdiction.

427
00:24:32,880 --> 00:24:37,440
And then if people have ideas about what is a good disclosure regime look like,

428
00:24:37,680 --> 00:24:39,920
what should the broker or dealer regime look like?

429
00:24:39,920 --> 00:24:43,800
Do you want to have a special purpose broker dealer framework or do you want

430
00:24:43,800 --> 00:24:47,120
to throw that out and just work with existing broker dealer framework?

431
00:24:47,120 --> 00:24:49,640
What does custody look like for investment advisors?

432
00:24:49,920 --> 00:24:51,680
How should we be thinking about that issue?

433
00:24:51,920 --> 00:24:56,680
So I do want people to talk to us if people have particular projects that

434
00:24:56,680 --> 00:24:58,920
they're trying to do and they want no action relief.

435
00:24:58,960 --> 00:25:00,880
I mean, our doors are open there as well.

436
00:25:01,040 --> 00:25:02,160
People need to be patient.

437
00:25:02,160 --> 00:25:05,640
It's going to take us a while to sort through all this, but we really do want

438
00:25:05,640 --> 00:25:06,600
to hear from people.

439
00:25:06,800 --> 00:25:10,680
I know that is going to take a while because people really feel they don't

440
00:25:10,680 --> 00:25:13,280
trust that they can really come in and talk to us.

441
00:25:13,280 --> 00:25:16,080
But I hope that gradually people will realize they can do that.

442
00:25:16,600 --> 00:25:16,920
Yeah.

443
00:25:17,040 --> 00:25:20,440
Well, I think the fact that you've stuck around and Commissioner Ueda really

444
00:25:20,440 --> 00:25:22,960
seems to be attuned to these same issues.

445
00:25:23,080 --> 00:25:23,760
Yeah, he is.

446
00:25:23,760 --> 00:25:26,960
Gives people a lot of comfort that it's not a trap this time.

447
00:25:27,120 --> 00:25:28,480
You're not going to step on my mind.

448
00:25:28,960 --> 00:25:32,840
I mean, I think that's part of the reason we need to move fast and try to get

449
00:25:32,960 --> 00:25:37,000
things in place so that people can really trust that this is going to last

450
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:40,240
also and Congress will be very helpful in that as well.

451
00:25:40,840 --> 00:25:44,960
Is there anything the industry is doing now that you would advise us to try to stop?

452
00:25:45,040 --> 00:25:47,840
I mean, there's certainly some bad behavior going out there.

453
00:25:48,080 --> 00:25:50,680
I worry about the self-inflicted wounds more than anything.

454
00:25:50,680 --> 00:25:55,000
This industry has shown an amazing propensity to just get in its own way over the years.

455
00:25:55,360 --> 00:25:56,440
Yeah, there are a couple of things.

456
00:25:56,440 --> 00:25:58,560
One, I would say don't commit fraud.

457
00:25:59,120 --> 00:25:59,760
Good start.

458
00:25:59,960 --> 00:26:05,520
Second, I would say remember the lessons from the traditional financial world.

459
00:26:05,880 --> 00:26:08,680
You don't get a pass on over leverage.

460
00:26:08,760 --> 00:26:12,960
You've got to think about what bad things could happen and prepare for those.

461
00:26:12,960 --> 00:26:16,560
And in this world, sometimes those things can happen much faster because it's just

462
00:26:16,560 --> 00:26:17,360
code working.

463
00:26:17,960 --> 00:26:22,120
If you do something, you can have a really negative consequence very quickly.

464
00:26:22,120 --> 00:26:23,120
So prepare for that.

465
00:26:23,200 --> 00:26:23,960
Think about that.

466
00:26:24,360 --> 00:26:26,440
But I mean, that's stuff that you guys all know.

467
00:26:26,440 --> 00:26:32,120
I think in terms of interacting with the SEC, remember that we've got limited resources

468
00:26:32,120 --> 00:26:33,720
and that we're trying to work through things.

469
00:26:33,720 --> 00:26:38,120
So when you brand something into us, something like you're trying to get an ETP through,

470
00:26:38,120 --> 00:26:42,160
remember that having it as buttoned down as possible is going to be helpful.

471
00:26:42,640 --> 00:26:47,040
I know everyone's trying to get in line first, but you have to think about the fact

472
00:26:47,040 --> 00:26:50,640
that if we've got to look through your filing and their technical problems with it,

473
00:26:50,640 --> 00:26:54,520
that's time taken away from working on a filing that actually is ready to go.

474
00:26:54,880 --> 00:26:58,360
And then my other piece of advice is just really basic.

475
00:26:58,440 --> 00:26:59,440
We iterate.

476
00:26:59,480 --> 00:27:02,400
That's how we have worked with traditional financial markets.

477
00:27:02,400 --> 00:27:06,960
So if you can say, OK, look, Hester, here's something that you guys did in the traditional

478
00:27:06,960 --> 00:27:07,760
financial markets.

479
00:27:07,760 --> 00:27:09,520
We're trying to take it one step further.

480
00:27:09,520 --> 00:27:13,000
We're trying to apply that same principle here in these markets.

481
00:27:13,480 --> 00:27:14,640
That's helpful to us.

482
00:27:15,000 --> 00:27:17,040
We can hang our hat on what we did before.

483
00:27:17,040 --> 00:27:20,640
And then we can say, look, what we're doing now is just an extension of that.

484
00:27:21,200 --> 00:27:24,280
And then being willing to have that conversation with us.

485
00:27:24,280 --> 00:27:29,360
If we ask questions, come back with answers and be willing to say, OK, SEC, if you're

486
00:27:29,360 --> 00:27:31,880
not wanting to move forward, explain to us why not.

487
00:27:31,880 --> 00:27:33,040
What's the sticking point?

488
00:27:33,400 --> 00:27:38,000
Be willing to press us when we say no to understand why we're saying no.

489
00:27:38,320 --> 00:27:40,600
That can get us further faster.

490
00:27:40,880 --> 00:27:42,040
That makes total sense.

491
00:27:42,520 --> 00:27:45,240
So from where I sit, it feels like kind of a new day.

492
00:27:45,600 --> 00:27:50,120
I am seeing investment firms that are starting to mobilize around, OK, maybe we'll

493
00:27:50,120 --> 00:27:52,000
move from an ERA to an RIA.

494
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:53,520
We're going to expand our strategy.

495
00:27:53,880 --> 00:27:56,720
Certainly seeing just a lot more startups get off the ground.

496
00:27:56,720 --> 00:28:00,000
It's been very, very busy since November over here.

497
00:28:00,280 --> 00:28:04,320
And a lot of interesting categories that you wouldn't necessarily have seen

498
00:28:04,480 --> 00:28:06,880
entrepreneur quality as high a couple of years ago.

499
00:28:06,920 --> 00:28:10,520
So people leaving big tech firms to start ambitious projects.

500
00:28:10,800 --> 00:28:14,360
Do you feel that energy is that visible to the SEC at all, that this is actually

501
00:28:14,360 --> 00:28:15,920
already having a positive effect?

502
00:28:16,480 --> 00:28:18,400
Yeah, I think I'm seeing glimmers of that.

503
00:28:18,800 --> 00:28:21,680
That is exactly what regulatory clarity can do.

504
00:28:21,680 --> 00:28:27,120
Because if you're asking someone to leave a comfortable job at an established

505
00:28:27,120 --> 00:28:31,840
company to start a venture only five years later to have the government

506
00:28:31,840 --> 00:28:36,040
basically shut that venture down and take away everything that you've been working

507
00:28:36,040 --> 00:28:37,760
on, people aren't going to do it.

508
00:28:37,960 --> 00:28:42,560
But if people know, OK, I know I'm going to run into some regulatory obstacles,

509
00:28:42,560 --> 00:28:46,000
but I'm going to have a regulator that I can work through those obstacles with.

510
00:28:46,040 --> 00:28:47,480
It's worth taking that risk.

511
00:28:48,080 --> 00:28:51,120
And that has always been the kind of country the US has been.

512
00:28:51,120 --> 00:28:54,840
It's that we've been a country that's rewarded people taking those risks,

513
00:28:55,200 --> 00:29:00,000
being willing to step away from a comfortable career into something new

514
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:01,200
to try something new.

515
00:29:01,200 --> 00:29:04,680
And we need to be the best country in the world to do that.

516
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:09,360
So I hope that in this little area where I'm working, I can help contribute to that.

517
00:29:10,040 --> 00:29:11,720
Well, I think that's a great place to leave it.

518
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:15,520
I guess I'll close by just saying thank you for all that you've done to bring

519
00:29:15,520 --> 00:29:16,920
some of these issues to light.

520
00:29:16,920 --> 00:29:21,040
It's often felt like no one was really understanding the plight of some of

521
00:29:21,040 --> 00:29:23,880
these entrepreneurs, so I really appreciate you hanging in there.

522
00:29:24,240 --> 00:29:29,040
Matt, I appreciate it and I appreciate what you all do to try to help us

523
00:29:29,040 --> 00:29:32,040
regulators and others understand what's going on in the industry.

524
00:29:32,480 --> 00:29:33,240
Well, thanks so much.

525
00:29:33,240 --> 00:29:34,520
We'd love to have you on again soon.

526
00:29:34,720 --> 00:29:35,320
Sounds good.

527
00:29:35,400 --> 00:29:35,880
Take care.

528
